From 4e9c0ca1aa4c18c6048da4d02ab230a0f988a51b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "A.Cervantez" <155030226+Ywach404@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 17:47:51 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] runtime: a shot can follow the aiming hand while the view
 follows the head

With head look the engine's view angles are the head's, so CreateMove copied them into every command and a shot went where the head looked, whatever the controller pointed at. A new m9 row (tools/m9/handaim.toml, cell 57) jumps out of CInput::CreateMove at client+0x1037a6, after SetViewAngles and ClientMode's CreateMove and before the command leaves, and the HandAim shim (runtime/handaim.c) turns the command's angles alone by the aiming hand's offset from the head pose those angles were written for (rt_headlook_hand_aim, with the head's yaw and pitch convention). The engine's angles, which the view is drawn from, are never written there, so the view stays the head's.

It is off unless RT_HANDAIM=1: on a headset run every shot went where the hand pointed with nothing on screen to aim by, and ARKit's aim for a Sense controller sat 18 to 34 degrees above where the hand pointed, so the aim is also turned down by RT_HANDAIM_TILT degrees (25). It acts on visionOS in first person head look in play, the game's live angles switch set and the aiming hand tracked, and logs the angles every 120 commands. build_all.py always adds the set, and the binding is made on every platform so a lift with the row never calls an unbound cell.
